# @PACK_NAME@

A complete installation set for the MSM8916 "Handsome OpenStick" 4G dongles (UFI001C /
UFI001B / UFI103S / UFI003 family). Built @BUILT@ for board **@BOARD_TAG@**.

Everything below was measured on real hardware, not inferred from vendor documentation. The
reasoning behind each step — and the five flash cycles that produced it — lives in the
repository's `BOARD-ufi001c.md`.

| | |
|---|---|
| result | Alpine Linux @ALPINE@ on mainline kernel **@KERNEL@** |
| default DTB | `@DEFAULT_DTB@` (alternates are already on the boot partition) |
| root PARTUUID | `@PARTUUID@` |
| rootfs image | @ROOTFS_BYTES@ bytes → grown with `resize2fs` on first boot |
| boot image | @BOOT_BYTES@ bytes, ext2 + `extlinux.conf` |
| factory images | @HAS_RESTORE@ |
| toolset | **@TOOLSET@** (minimal = editor + ssh client; full adds nmap/tcpdump/bind-tools/iperf3/mtr/…) |
| pack version | v@REPO_VERSION@ |

## What you need on the host

- `fastboot` (Android platform-tools). On Debian/Ubuntu it works without sudo if
  `/lib/udev/rules.d/51-android.rules` exists and your user is in `plugdev`.
- `sha256sum` (coreutils) — the installers verify the pack before writing anything.
- `lsusb` (usbutils) — used to detect the board coming back.
- Roughly 15 minutes. The rootfs transfer alone is ~6.5 min for ~940 MB (sparse-trimmed: only
  the ~222 MB that is not zero is actually sent).
- If the stick will not boot at all, there is a second door: EDL. It needs the firehose
  programmer (`vendor/db410c-firmware/prog_emmc_firehose_8916.mbn`) and, on Debian,
  `python3-usb pyserial docopt colorama` + bkerler/edl. `scripts/78-flash-over-edl.sh` in the
  repository wraps it, and unlike fastboot it can also read partitions back — so it can prove a
  write without booting it. See the recovery section.

## Before you start

**This pack's `restore/` images came from one specific board.** They are the factory
`aboot`/`hyp`/`sbl1`/`rpm`/`tz`/`boot` of the unit this pack was built for. Restoring them
onto a *different* unit writes that other unit's bootloader — same family, same partition
layout, but not its own images. If the board in front of you is not @BOARD_TAG@, back it up
first (`scripts/20-backup.sh`) and rebuild the pack for it.

Read the whole file before flashing. In particular: `fastboot fetch` is not implemented by
either bootloader on these boards, so **nothing can be read back after a write**. The only
verification available is booting it, which is what the installer does at each rung.

## Install

```sh
./flash.sh                       # dry run: verifies the manifest, prints the plan
COMMIT=1 I_WIPE_ROOTFS=yes ./flash.sh
```

Get into fastboot first. From a running system, no hands needed:

```sh
adb reboot bootloader            # vendor Debian image
scripts/72-enter-fastboot.sh       # Alpine image (reboot(2) RESTART2 over ssh)
```

From a dead system the button is **not** a way into fastboot: measured on the UFI103s, holding
it while plugging in puts the board into EDL (`05c6:9008`, QDL), which needs the `edl` tool and
a firehose programmer (`vendor/db410c-firmware/prog_emmc_firehose_8916.mbn`). It is the last
door, not the easy one.

### Why the installer goes in this order

1. **rootfs first, bootloader untouched.** The Alpine rootfs carries the vendor 5.15 modules
   and `/lib/firmware`, so the *stock* Android `boot` image boots it unchanged. After this
   rung the board runs a new userspace on a known-good kernel — the reversible checkpoint.
   That checkpoint only exists if the image's ext4 features are ones the **vendor** kernel can
   mount read-write. `mke2fs` 1.47 sets `metadata_csum_seed` by default, which needs Linux 6.3;
   measured on the UFI103s, a rootfs carrying it made the stock 5.15.0 initrd fail to mount root
   and the board went completely silent on USB (no adb, no gadget, no fastboot) — a failure this
   rung is not supposed to be able to produce. `scripts/53-build-alpine-cross.sh` refuses to
   emit such an image.
2. **Then `aboot` + `hyp`.** The factory `aboot` has no `extlinux` parser at all
   (`grep -c extlinux` over it returns 0) and will not start a mainline arm64 kernel: three
   different 6.12 payloads all fell back to fastboot, while a byte-identical no-op repack of
   the stock image booted. The community `lk1st` parses `extlinux.conf` and runs @KERNEL@.
   Rebooting between rungs proves the new aboot still boots the legacy image before anything
   depends on it.
3. **Then `boot`.** Only now is an ext2 + `extlinux.conf` partition readable. Flashing this
   image before rung 2 leaves the board in fastboot with no OS — that is the single most
   common way to get this wrong.

`sbl1`, `rpm` and `tz` are **never written** by the installer. This board's `tz` is already
byte-identical to the Dragon Board 410c one, and @KERNEL@ boots with the vendor `sbl1`/`rpm`
in place — so the riskiest flash on this SoC is simply not needed. The calibration partitions
(`modemst1`, `modemst2`, `fsg`, `fsc`, `sec`, `cdt`, `devinfo`) carry the radio identity and
are not written by anything in this pack either.

## First boot

```sh
ip -br addr | grep -i enx        # a 192.168.5.x lease = the USB gadget came up
adb devices                      # the board's own serial, not a placeholder
adb -s <serial> shell id         # uid=0(root)
```

Then, over `adb shell` or `ssh -i <key> root@192.168.5.1`:

```sh
resize2fs /dev/disk/by-partlabel/rootfs      # the image is ~896 MB, the partition is 3.3 GiB
passwd user                                   # upstream's default user/1 - change it
```

Login reality, corrected 2026-11-06 by reading the image rather than the docs: **`root` is
locked** (`passwd -S root` → `L`) and there is no `authorized_keys`, so
`ssh -i <key> root@192.168.5.1` from this file's earlier revision could never have worked. The
account is `user` with the builder's default password and passwordless sudo.

`dropbear` is **not enabled** in this image. An ssh server whose only credential is a
published default password should be opened on purpose:

```sh
rc-update add dropbear default && rc-service dropbear start     # then: ssh user@192.168.5.1
```

Wi-Fi is a client decision, not a flashed one — no network and no password is baked in:

```sh
nmcli device wifi list
nmcli device wifi connect "<SSID>" password "<PSK>"
nat-share.sh status                    # what it is sharing to the host, and why
```

Three admin doors exist, and it is worth confirming all three before you need one:

| door | how | survives |
|---|---|---|
| adb | `adb -s <serial> shell` (root, binary-safe push/pull) | a broken network config |
| ssh over NCM | `ssh user@192.168.5.1` — **opt-in**, see above; or the Wi-Fi/LTE address | a broken USB gadget |
| USB serial | `cu -l /dev/ttyACM0 -s 115200` → passwordless root shell | both of the above |

`adb exec-out` answers `error: closed` on this daemon; use `adb shell` for text and
`adb pull`/`push` for binaries.

## Switching kernel or DTB — no reflash needed

`/boot` is a real mounted ext2 partition, so this is a file copy:

```sh
ls /boot/extlinux/
cp /boot/extlinux/extlinux-handsome.conf /boot/extlinux/extlinux.conf && reboot   # stock DTB
cp /boot/extlinux/extlinux-5.15.conf     /boot/extlinux/extlinux.conf && reboot   # vendor 5.15
```

The community DTB is the default because it carries the `rmtfs@`/`mpss@` reserved-memory
nodes the modem probe needs; under the stock `handsome,openstick` DTB the modem fails with
`qcom-q6v5-mss: unable to resolve mpss region` and there is no LTE at all. The stock DTB's
advantage is that it describes the dual-SIM mux, which the community one collapses to a
single external slot.

## Recovery

```sh
./restore.sh                                  # dry run
COMMIT=1 I_RESTORE_FACTORY=yes ./restore.sh   # aboot/hyp/sbl1/rpm/tz/boot back to factory
```

That restores the **boot chain only**. The rootfs partition is not recoverable from this
pack: there is no raw image of the original filesystem, because reading one back needs
`fastboot fetch` (unimplemented) or EDL (unproven on this family). What the repository keeps
instead is a file-level archive, so a full userspace rollback means creating a fresh ext4 on
the `rootfs` partition and unpacking that archive into it.

Last resort, if the board shows `05c6:9008` on USB: it is in EDL and needs a firehose
programmer (`prog_emmc_firehose_8916.mbn`) plus the `edl` tool.

## Provenance

Every file in this pack is listed in `MANIFEST.sha256`, which both installers verify with
`sha256sum -c --strict` before writing anything. The pack also carries `adbd-payload/`, the
three files that give a musl rootfs a root `adb shell` — the vendor's glibc `adbd` running
under `gcompat`, plus the `libcrypto.so.1.1` it needs (which cannot be replaced by Alpine's
`libcrypto.so.3`, because the daemon references the *versioned* symbol
`RSA_verify@OPENSSL_1_1_0`). `scripts/76-fetch-adbd-payload.sh` in the repository re-derives
and re-verifies them.
